WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.839
- You snooze, you win.

00:00:02.839 --> 00:00:04.922
(laughs)

00:00:06.894 --> 00:00:10.240
- I am very known to oversleep.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.840
I hit snooze all the time.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.560
- That extra nine minutes,
two minutes, is just glorious.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.962
- When I wake up in the morning

00:00:16.962 --> 00:00:19.030
and I know that I have to go to work

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.930
I just want to curl up in a ball and die.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.120
- I will also set multiple alarms

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.560
and this Sleeping Beauty is not having it.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.480
No matter how determined
I am when I go to bed.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:29.860
- Snoozing for life, baby.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.556
- You're telling me this is bad for us?

00:00:31.556 --> 00:00:32.815
- [Woman Off Camera] I'm
going to have you guys read

00:00:32.815 --> 00:00:33.770
some facts about snoozing.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:34.750
They're not great...

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.060
- [Narrator] Snoozing
confuses your body and mind

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.720
about whether or not it's time to get up.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.830
This may cause "sleep inertia,"

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.020
which can leave you groggy

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.871
for up to four hours after waking.

00:00:43.871 --> 00:00:45.135
- That makes a lot of sense.

00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:46.317
- Isn't that what coffee's for?

00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:47.150
Right?

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:48.083
To get you going?

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:49.160
- I don't drink coffee.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.400
- I do feel this, like,
lagging sense of I'm tired.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.480
- I really don't feel my body waking up

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.410
until like 1:00 or 2:00.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.731
So I guess this fact kinda feels like

00:00:58.731 --> 00:01:01.160
it's very accurate when
it comes to my life.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.364
- I feel totally fine and
I snoozed this morning.

00:01:03.364 --> 00:01:04.610
Totally fine.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.603
- [Narrator] Snoozing throws
off your internal clock,

00:01:06.603 --> 00:01:10.400
causing your body not to
know when to become sleepy,

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.010
pushing your bedtime later,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.020
and leading to more snoozing.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.490
- Hmm, that's interesting

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.760
'cause you know I actually
have trouble falling asleep.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.940
- My internal clock is fucked up anyway

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.198
because you know during the week

00:01:21.198 --> 00:01:23.199
we have to wake up early to get to work.

00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:26.560
And on the weekends I'll go out late

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.520
and be out with friends,
drinking or hanging out

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.126
or doing whatever and then, like,

00:01:31.126 --> 00:01:33.630
I'll be in bed 'til 11 or noon.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.380
- I wish I had an earlier bedtime.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.360
I have trouble with that.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.140
So maybe the snoozing is affecting it.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.300
But I doubt it.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.640
- [Narrator] Messing
up your internal clocks

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.944
makes for restless nights,

00:01:42.944 --> 00:01:46.500
and sleep deprivation can
screw up hundreds of genes

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.402
that regulate your immune
system, stress response

00:01:50.402 --> 00:01:51.737
and metabolism.

00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:53.173
- Wow, that took a turn.

00:01:53.173 --> 00:01:55.370
I don't know why I was
reading that so joyfully,

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:56.640
that's a bummer.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.920
- I'm not a scientist, but no.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.030
- My immune system definitely
got worse over the years

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.420
and it's funny 'cause as I've gotten older

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.460
I've gotten less sleep.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.750
- Maybe that's why I'm always stressed?

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.650
- I've noticed the
nights where I got, like,

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.210
a shitty night's sleep or
like snoozed a lot, whatever,

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:10.810
that I was more anxious and stressed out

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:11.780
at work the next day.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.050
- I think the concept of me
somehow snoozing my alarm clock

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.967
changing my genes is scary,

00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:21.720
but because it's so scary, I
choose not to believe that.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.819
I don't think it's true.

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:25.826
- Screwing up genes like a sale at Macy's.

00:02:25.826 --> 00:02:28.810
- [Narrator] Scientists
believe these genetic changes

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.910
can potentially increase
your risk of heart disease,

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.641
weight gain or diabetes.

00:02:33.641 --> 00:02:35.570
- I feel like I need
to be in the hospital.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.040
Like somebody call me
an ambulance right now.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.348
- Doesn't everything cause you to die?

00:02:38.348 --> 00:02:39.390
- Yeah.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.223
- Right?

00:02:40.223 --> 00:02:42.642
- We're all slowly dying from all things.

00:02:42.642 --> 00:02:44.247
So, like, who cares?

00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:46.130
- If your friends want
to hang out with you

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.290
and you want to go to sleep

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.820
and they make you feel bad about it.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.850
Just be, like, you're very
much impacting my health

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.609
and future so let me sleep.

00:02:53.609 --> 00:02:55.990
- You're giving me heart disease, Dennis.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.280
That's what you're doing.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.686
- [Narrator] According to
neuroscientist Matthew Walker,

00:02:59.686 --> 00:03:02.778
hitting snooze causes
"cardiovascular assault"

00:03:02.778 --> 00:03:04.332
on your heart each time it goes off.

00:03:04.332 --> 00:03:05.480
- Oh my gosh.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.360
- Somebody call the police.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.570
- Square up, I guess.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.180
- Bitch, I believe it's
a mini heart attack.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.580
Every time I hear it, every
time I hear my alarm go off

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.180
I jump four feet into the air.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.610
- I'm really scared about hitting snooze.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.900
I feel like next time I hit snooze

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.449
I'm going to see my life
flash before my eyes.

00:03:17.449 --> 00:03:18.282
(laughs)

00:03:18.282 --> 00:03:20.370
And I'm going to be
like alright, it's fine,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.203
Jesus, I'll get up.

00:03:21.203 --> 00:03:24.710
- I feel like neuroscientists
are trying to tell me

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.280
how to live my life when
they don't even know me.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.509
- I wish I was more scared

00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:30.803
but I'm still going to
continue to hit snooze.

00:03:30.803 --> 00:03:34.160
Like, honestly, I mean,
it's been a pattern

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.740
and an on-going thing for years.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.797
How am I going to stop
even with these facts?

00:03:37.797 --> 00:03:39.562
- In a perfect world,

00:03:39.562 --> 00:03:42.150
I wouldn't have to wake
up in the morning, like,

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.796
I could just sleep until my body is ready.

00:03:44.796 --> 00:03:47.930
And wouldn't that be like the
healthiest thing for my body.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.460
But I have to make money.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.170
I have to live, neuroscientists.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.500
- You have to live in this society.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.210
- But it's surprising
like the severity of stuff

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.660
that it can lead to and overall

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.800
I'm still inclined to think
it's not like that big of a deal

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.191
and I'm still going to face
my own internal struggle

00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:04.110
of waking up to get to the gym anyway.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.809
- Keep snoozing until the day I die.

00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:08.470
And then that day I can't snooze.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:09.550
- Snooze til you die.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.383
- Yeah.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:11.216
- Just do the best you can.

00:04:11.216 --> 00:04:12.090
- Yeah.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.490
- In this lifetime what more can you do?

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.120
- If you're a snoozer you're not a loser.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:16.953
That's all I gotta say.

00:04:16.953 --> 00:04:17.786
- I like that.

00:04:17.786 --> 00:04:19.720
I want a shirt that says that.

